body: "## Problem\n\n`src/services/pm-runner.ts` executes the pm CLI with `spawnSync` (also `initProject` and extension discovery). Every pm invocation therefore blocks the whole Node event loop for the full CLI runtime (~0.6–0.9 s per mutation in practice).\n\nConsequences for multi-user deployments:\n\n- While any pm command runs, **no** other request makes progress — including requests for unrelated projects/tenants.\n- SSE writes and `pg_notify` LISTEN callbacks (`realtime-bus.ts`) are also stalled. In an authenticated load test (1000–2000 SSE clients on one shared project), a burst of concurrent creates delayed cross-process invalidation delivery by ~9 s; in a heavier run the events arrived only after a 10 s measurement window. Events are delayed, not lost, and same-process broadcasts flush as soon as the loop unblocks — but real-time freshness degrades exactly when the system is busiest.\n- Debounced Neo4j graph syncs (`scheduleGraphSync` → more pm CLI calls) amplify the effect after every mutation burst.\n\nMeasured context (same load run): the SSE layer itself is healthy — 2000/2000 clients connected, 100 % event delivery, fan-out spread within a round is only tens of ms thanks to per-project indexing, zero drops over a soak. The synchronous runner is now the dominant concurrency ceiling.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nConvert `runPm` (and `initProject`/extension discovery) to async `spawn`/`execFile` returning a Promise, and `await` at call sites (route handlers are already async; ~85 call sites in `routes/pm.ts` are mechanical rewrites). Per-workspace serialization is already guaranteed by the pm CLI workspace lock, so allowing cross-project parallelism is safe and desirable. Optionally cap global concurrency with a small semaphore to bound child-process fan-out.\n\n## Repro sketch\n\n1. Open N SSE clients on a project (`GET /api/projects/:id/pm/events`).\n2. Fire a dozen concurrent `POST .../pm/create` requests.\n3. Publish a foreign-`sourceId` `pg_notify` on `pm_workspace_events` during the burst and time its SSE delivery — it arrives seconds late, while at idle it takes <100 ms.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)"
